1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,017
Aarav picks a colour in a
drawing app on his phone.

2
00:00:03,317 --> 00:00:06,920
The colour code reads FF,
eight eight, zero zero.

3
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:08,895
Letters inside a number?

4
00:00:08,895 --> 00:00:12,313
And his phone only knows
two digits, zero and one.

5
00:00:12,813 --> 00:00:14,498
Let's start with how we count.

6
00:00:14,498 --> 00:00:16,435
We use ten digits, zero to nine.

7
00:00:16,785 --> 00:00:18,943
After nine, we run out of digits.

8
00:00:18,943 --> 00:00:20,504
So we write one, zero.

9
00:00:20,504 --> 00:00:21,404
Ten.

10
00:00:21,309 --> 00:00:24,698
The number of digits a system
uses is called its base.

11
00:00:24,698 --> 00:00:25,956
Ours is base ten.

12
00:00:26,606 --> 00:00:28,487
Now think of a light switch.

13
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:30,078
It has only two states.

14
00:00:30,428 --> 00:00:31,569
Off, or on.

15
00:00:31,569 --> 00:00:32,817
Zero, or one.

16
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:36,038
A wire in a computer works the same way.

17
00:00:36,038 --> 00:00:37,572
Low voltage is zero.

18
00:00:37,922 --> 00:00:39,466
High voltage is one.

19
00:00:39,816 --> 00:00:42,048
So computers count in base two.

20
00:00:42,048 --> 00:00:43,960
This is the binary system.

21
00:00:44,610 --> 00:00:46,805
One binary digit is called a bit.

22
00:00:46,805 --> 00:00:48,801
Bit is short for binary digit.

23
00:00:49,451 --> 00:00:51,238
Let's count on the breadboard.

24
00:00:51,238 --> 00:00:53,160
Four switches, and four lamps.

25
00:00:53,510 --> 00:00:55,094
Each switch is one bit.

26
00:00:55,094 --> 00:00:56,101
On means one.

27
00:00:56,101 --> 00:00:57,200
Off means zero.

28
00:00:57,550 --> 00:01:01,378
In decimal, each place is worth
ten times the place on its right.

29
00:01:01,728 --> 00:01:04,198
In binary, each place is worth two times.

30
00:01:04,198 --> 00:01:05,819
One, two, four, eight.

31
00:01:06,169 --> 00:01:07,886
Switch on the first bit.

32
00:01:07,886 --> 00:01:09,200
The value is one.

33
00:01:09,550 --> 00:01:11,295
Now the two and the one.

34
00:01:11,295 --> 00:01:12,886
Two plus one is three.

35
00:01:13,236 --> 00:01:14,910
Now eight, two and one.

36
00:01:14,910 --> 00:01:17,114
Eight plus two plus one is eleven.

37
00:01:17,464 --> 00:01:20,786
The scope shows the same
bits as high and low pulses.

38
00:01:21,136 --> 00:01:22,965
All four on gives fifteen.

39
00:01:22,965 --> 00:01:25,662
So four bits count from zero to fifteen.

40
00:01:26,312 --> 00:01:28,506
The left bit has the biggest weight.

41
00:01:28,506 --> 00:01:30,424
It is the most significant bit.

42
00:01:30,774 --> 00:01:33,000
The right bit has the smallest weight.

43
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:34,915
It is the least significant bit.

44
00:01:35,565 --> 00:01:36,917
Now the other way.

45
00:01:36,917 --> 00:01:39,318
How do we turn thirteen into binary?

46
00:01:39,668 --> 00:01:41,719
Divide by two again and again.

47
00:01:41,719 --> 00:01:43,459
Write down each remainder.

48
00:01:44,159 --> 00:01:47,582
Thirteen divided by two
is six, remainder one.

49
00:01:48,282 --> 00:01:51,691
Six divided by two is
three, remainder zero.

50
00:01:52,391 --> 00:01:55,631
Three divided by two
is one, remainder one.

51
00:01:56,331 --> 00:01:59,449
One divided by two is zero, remainder one.

52
00:01:59,449 --> 00:02:00,598
Stop at zero.

53
00:02:01,298 --> 00:02:03,495
Now read the remainders
from the bottom up.

54
00:02:03,495 --> 00:02:04,861
One, one, zero, one.

55
00:02:05,561 --> 00:02:06,461
Check it.

56
00:02:06,253 --> 00:02:08,380
Eight plus four plus one is thirteen.

57
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:11,670
Long rows of ones and
zeros are hard to read.

58
00:02:11,670 --> 00:02:12,938
So we use shortcuts.

59
00:02:13,288 --> 00:02:14,656
Octal is base eight.

60
00:02:14,656 --> 00:02:16,785
It uses the digits zero to seven.

61
00:02:17,135 --> 00:02:19,440
Eight is two times two times two.

62
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,895
So one octal digit holds three bits.

63
00:02:22,545 --> 00:02:24,506
Hexadecimal is base sixteen.

64
00:02:24,506 --> 00:02:26,260
It needs sixteen digits.

65
00:02:26,610 --> 00:02:28,794
After nine, it borrows letters.

66
00:02:28,794 --> 00:02:31,415
A is ten, B is eleven, and so on.

67
00:02:31,765 --> 00:02:32,829
F is fifteen.

68
00:02:32,829 --> 00:02:35,115
Sixteen is two to the power four.

69
00:02:35,465 --> 00:02:37,695
So one hex digit holds four bits.

70
00:02:38,345 --> 00:02:39,911
Four bits make a nibble.

71
00:02:39,911 --> 00:02:41,431
Eight bits make a byte.

72
00:02:41,781 --> 00:02:45,535
So FF is one one one one, one one one one.

73
00:02:45,885 --> 00:02:47,922
That is two hundred and fifty five.

74
00:02:48,272 --> 00:02:51,056
That is why the colour code has letters.

75
00:02:51,056 --> 00:02:53,371
Each pair is one byte, in hex.

76
00:02:54,021 --> 00:02:55,798
Two digits can count anything.

77
00:02:55,798 --> 00:02:57,627
Groups make them easy to read.

78
00:02:58,427 --> 00:02:59,544
Worked example.

79
00:02:59,544 --> 00:03:03,394
Write one hundred and fifty
six in binary, octal and hex.

80
00:03:03,744 --> 00:03:07,253
Divide by two again and again,
and read the remainders upward.

81
00:03:07,603 --> 00:03:11,147
We get one zero zero
one, one one zero zero.

82
00:03:11,847 --> 00:03:13,106
Check the weights.

83
00:03:13,106 --> 00:03:16,696
One twenty eight, plus
sixteen, plus eight, plus four.

84
00:03:16,696 --> 00:03:17,766
One fifty six.

85
00:03:18,466 --> 00:03:20,994
For octal, group three
bits from the right.

86
00:03:20,994 --> 00:03:22,250
Two, three, four.

87
00:03:22,950 --> 00:03:25,614
For hex, group four bits from the right.

88
00:03:25,614 --> 00:03:26,677
Nine, and C.

89
00:03:27,377 --> 00:03:31,992
So one fifty six is two three
four in octal, and nine C in hex.

90
00:03:32,642 --> 00:03:34,104
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:03:34,454 --> 00:03:36,743
Many students read the
remainders from top to bottom.

92
00:03:37,093 --> 00:03:39,966
For thirteen, that gives one zero one one.

93
00:03:39,966 --> 00:03:42,025
That is eleven, not thirteen.

94
00:03:42,675 --> 00:03:44,483
Always read the remainders upward.

95
00:03:44,483 --> 00:03:46,758
And always group bits from the right side.

96
00:03:47,408 --> 00:03:49,031
Where is this asked?

97
00:03:49,031 --> 00:03:50,895
In PUC board papers.

98
00:03:51,245 --> 00:03:54,938
And in ISC Computer
Science, in class eleven.

99
00:03:55,288 --> 00:03:59,722
Expect quick conversions between
binary, octal, decimal and hex.

100
00:04:00,372 --> 00:04:01,272
Try this.

101
00:04:01,208 --> 00:04:04,981
What is the hexadecimal number
two F, written in octal?

102
00:04:10,381 --> 00:04:11,281
C.

103
00:04:10,784 --> 00:04:11,690
Five seven.

104
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:14,227
Write each hex digit as four bits.

105
00:04:14,577 --> 00:04:16,634
Two is zero zero one zero.

106
00:04:16,634 --> 00:04:18,421
F is one one one one.

107
00:04:18,771 --> 00:04:21,165
Now regroup in threes from the right.

108
00:04:21,165 --> 00:04:23,323
One zero one, and one one one.

109
00:04:23,673 --> 00:04:25,198
That is five and seven.

110
00:04:25,548 --> 00:04:28,677
Check: five eights plus
seven is forty seven.

111
00:04:28,677 --> 00:04:30,566
Two F is forty seven too.

112
00:04:31,216 --> 00:04:32,763
Screenshot this card.

113
00:04:35,963 --> 00:04:37,296
Next, logic gates.

114
00:04:37,296 --> 00:04:39,515
How does a chip decide yes or no?
